14
01:11:07,199 --> 01:11:13,199
The Day After is a 1983 American television film directed by Nicholas Meyer.

15
01:16:12,000 --> 01:16:18,000
The war film postulates a fictional conflict between NATO and the Warsaw Pact over Germany that rapidly
escalates into a full-scale nuclear exchange between the United States and the Soviet Union.

16
01:21:16,800 --> 01:21:22,800
The action itself focuses on the residents of Lawrence, Kansas, and Kansas City,
Missouri, and several family farms near American missile silos.

17
01:26:21,600 --> 01:26:27,600
The cast includes JoBeth Williams, Steve Guttenberg, John
Cullum, Jason Robards, and John Lithgow.

18
01:31:26,400 --> 01:31:32,400
The film was written by Edward Hume and produced by Robert Papazian.

19
01:36:31,199 --> 01:36:37,199
More than 100 million people, in nearly 39 million households, watched the film when
it first aired on November 20, 1983, on the ABC television network.

20
01:41:36,000 --> 01:41:42,000
With a 46 rating and a 62% share of the viewing audience during the initial broadcast, the film was the seventh-highest-rated
non-sports show until then, and in a 2009 Nielsen TV Ratings list was one of the highest-rated television films in US history.

21
01:46:40,800 --> 01:46:46,800
The film was broadcast on Soviet state television in 1987, during the
negotiations on Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty.

22
01:51:45,600 --> 01:51:51,600
The producers demanded that the Russian translation conform to the original
script and that the broadcast not be interrupted by commentary.

23
01:56:50,400 --> 01:56:56,400
The film explores themes of high school, environmental
protection agency, nuclear war, isolation, darkness.

24
02:01:55,200 --> 02:02:01,200
The tone is dark and thought_provoking and emotional.
